# openElement Showcase

Generated by [`@openelement/create`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@openelement/create) —
scaffold it elsewhere with `npm create @openelement <project-name>` —
a static-first openElement app that shows its own costs. The landing page
carries two islands (`my-counter`, an interactive counter, and `live-timer`,
a client-only elapsed-time readout); every other page, including `/about`,
is prerendered HTML and CSS with no islands of its own — no island chunk is
executed there (a linked page's chunks can still be prefetched); the only
script such a page executes is the app-wide claim bootstrap (about 2 KB
gzipped) that runs while the app has any island. The badge on each section
names what it costs the client.

Docs and guides: https://openelement.org

Requires **Node.js 24.2+ (24.18.0 recommended)** and any one package manager:
npm, pnpm, yarn, or bun. Node 24.2 is the floor the packed framework packages
declare in their `engines` and CI exercises; 24.18.0 is the line this template
is verified on, pinned by the scaffold's own `.node-version`. The generated
project pins no package manager: every script is a plain command, so whichever
manager you install with runs them all.

One directory is generated rather than source: `.openElement/` at the project
root holds the build's internal state (head-convention bookkeeping and build
plans). The dev server and the build write it as needed; the `.gitignore`
excludes it and deleting it is safe — the next run recreates it.

The `@openelement/element` and `@openelement/router` dependencies carry caret
ranges over the `1.0.0` prerelease this starter was generated from, so a
dependency update picks up later `1.0.0` prereleases (and the final `1.0.0`)
without editing this file.

Nothing here is tied to one package manager: the manifest declares no
`packageManager`, every script is a plain command, and the scaffold ships no
`pnpm-workspace.yaml`. Install with npm, pnpm, yarn, or bun — whichever one
runs first writes its lockfile, and every command below works the same.

## The JS cost map

| Surface                              | Ships JavaScript?     | Why                                               |
| ------------------------------------ | --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| `/` hero + architecture sections     | no                    | prerendered markup, styled with CSS               |
| `/` counter island (`my-counter`)    | yes, hydrates on idle | the one interactive piece                         |
| `/` live timer island (`live-timer`) | yes, client-only      | a live clock has nothing to prerender             |
| `/about`                             | no islands of its own | fully static SSG output, no island chunk executes |
| `/api/ping`                          | no (server)           | a plain `Request → Response` handler              |
| `/404` (unmatched paths)             | no (server)           | dynamic 404 from the styled 404 page              |

The badge on each section is a Tailwind utility string on the span itself
(`bg-ok/8 text-ok` for a static section, `bg-brand-soft text-brand` for an
interactive one) — copy the span onto new sections and keep the map honest.

> Cost honesty: the island client is emitted app-wide today, so a page
> without islands (like `/about`) still carries the ~2 KB gzipped app-wide
> claim bootstrap — but no island chunk executes and no element-runtime
> preload is added for it: the build records islands per page and the client
> only activates tags the page hosts. Fetches can still be broader than
> execution when the reader follows a link, because the target page's chunks
> may be prefetched; what a page executes is always limited to its own
> registered islands.

## Where things live

Framework options have exactly one home: `openelement.config.ts`. It is
optional and nearly empty — every option it omits comes from a file
convention:

| Option                | Convention                                                          |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| design tokens         | `app/styles/theme.css` (the @theme role sheet)                      |
| layout                | `app/layout.tsx` (the shared site chrome every page renders inside) |
| document head content | `app/head.tsx` (meta tags, preloads, inline CSS)                    |
| site title            | the `name` field of `package.json`                                  |

Passing framework options inline to `openElement()` while the config file
carries options is a hard error, and an unknown key fails the build with the
accepted-key list. `vite.config.ts` stays Vite-only: `plugins: [...openElement()]`.

- `app/routes/` — page routes, one file per URL. The default renderIntent is
  `static`: prerendered at build time (SSG). `/about` and `/` are static.
- `app/routes/api/` — request-time server routes. `/api/ping` is a plain
  WinterCG-shaped handler returning `Response.json`.
- `app/components/` — compiled page elements. Presentation is authored as
  Tailwind utility classes in the markup; each element's `.css` sheet carries
  only its root's box rule, which has no markup to carry it.
- `app/islands/` — client islands, the only code that ships JavaScript. Each
  island declares its hydration strategy (`idle`, `load`, `visible`,
  client-only) through `defineIslandConfig`.
- `app/layout.tsx` — the auto-registered layout, not an island itself: the
  site chrome every page renders inside. The header, the `main` content
  block, and the footer live here exactly once instead of being copied onto
  each page. The build registers the layout automatically and projects each
  page's render into its default slot, so a page module carries only its own
  content. The layout is a convention: delete the file and pages still render
  (carrying their own chrome again), or opt out explicitly with `layout: false`
  in `openelement.config.ts`.
- `app/styles/theme.css` — the @theme role sheet: Tailwind-ON roles, the
  starter palette (`bg-paper`, `text-brand`, `border-line`, …), dark and
  forced-colors pairs, and the document base (`body`, `::selection`). Dev
  serves the compiled result at `/.openElement/tailwind.css` and the build
  emits it as the content-hashed document stylesheet the head links — both
  through the official `@tailwindcss/vite` pipeline.
  Dark values ride `prefers-color-scheme`; no theme runtime.
- `app/head.tsx` — structural head content the URL list cannot express.

## Scripts

Every script is a plain command, so every one of them runs under any package
manager (`npm run dev`, `pnpm run dev`, `yarn run dev`, `bun run dev` — this
file spells them as `npm run …`):

- `npm run dev` — start the Vite dev server (`oe dev`).
- `npm run fmt` — format the sources (`oxfmt`).
- `npm run lint` — lint the sources (`oxlint`).
- `npm run typecheck` — type-check every route/component file (`tsc --noEmit`).
  The check covers the whole `app/` directory (`tsconfig.json`), so new files
  are picked up automatically.
- `npm test` — run Node's built-in test runner (the starter ships none).
- `npm run build` — production build into `dist/`: prerendered HTML for every
  static route, plus the request-time server when the app has one.
- `npm start` — serve the production build.

`dev`, `build`, and `start` run the router CLI's short `oe` bin from the
`@openelement/router` dependency (`oe dev` / `oe build` / `oe start`; the same
entry is installed as `openelement`, so both spellings work). `oe dev` boots the
framework's own dev server — the SSR dev middleware, HMR, and the Tailwind
pipeline — with no extra wrapper, and `oe start --mode=preview` is the
static-only serve mode that refuses a build carrying a request-time server.

`oxfmt` and `oxlint` run with their defaults: both skip markdown, JSON, YAML,
and CSS, so `npx oxfmt --check` and `npx oxlint` pass on a fresh scaffold.

The Vite tree is one direct devDependency pin (`"vite": "8.1.6"` — the same
version `@openelement/router` resolves against), so every peer that asks for
Vite gets that copy. There is no workspace catalog and no `vite@*` override:
a pin plus the router's own aligned dependency is what keeps the tree single.

### Package manager

Install with whichever manager you prefer; the first install decides the
lockfile and everything afterwards follows it. The manifest deliberately
carries no `packageManager` field: with the field absent, the manager you run
first is the one that owns the project. The commands in this file are spelled
`npm run …` only because npm's spelling works unchanged under all four.

## Styling ownership

Styling is utility-first: presentation is authored as Tailwind utility
classes directly in the markup, and nothing else in the app spells it out.
The compiler admits those static tokens and the build generates the CSS — the
document stylesheet for light-root pages and the layout, one shared component
stylesheet for the shadow-root islands — so every page is fully styled with
zero client JavaScript and no CSS text ships inside JavaScript.

Two things stay CSS, and each has a reason:

- `app/styles/theme.css` — the design tokens and the document base (`body`,
  `::selection`, the dark and forced-colors pairs). The document shell's
  `body` is not authored markup, so no class can carry it.
- the per-element `.css` sheet — one `:host` rule for the element's own box.
  A custom element is `display: inline` by default and the host tag is not
  authored markup either.

The `.css` + `static styles` authoring path stays fully available — array any
sheet you want and the server inlines it into the SSR output as a
`@scope(<tag>)` block — the starter simply has no need for it.

