## Dependency notes

`@openelement/element` and `@openelement/router` are the runtime dependencies
your app imports. Everything else this manifest declares rides devDependencies:
`vite` (the tree the router's `oe dev` / `oe build` drive), `oxfmt` and `oxlint`
(the `fmt` / `lint` scripts), the Tailwind-ON build-time pins, the dev-server
pair — `@hono/vite-dev-server` (loaded lazily by the dev server) and its `hono`
peer — and the `typescript` pin.

`npm install --omit=dev` drops what this manifest declares as devDependencies —
the Tailwind pair, `oxfmt`, `oxlint`, the Vite pin, `hono` — but it does not
shrink the install to the runtime pair: `@openelement/router` declares its own
dependencies (the compiler, the protocol package, TypeScript, PostCSS, `mime`,
`pathe`) and npm installs those in a production install too. Vite, Element,
`hono`, `marked` and `@hono/vite-dev-server` are optional peers of the router
and are never auto-installed, so a `--omit=dev` tree has no Vite: run the build
from the full install and ship only its output.

The generated server entry itself imports no HTTP framework — it composes on
the internal WinterCG layer — and `hono` is only an optional peer of
`@openelement/router`: `@openelement/router/hono` (`createHonoAdapter`) is the
one opt-in if you ever want to mount the built handler inside your own Hono
app. For Nitro deployments, the built handler bridges through the
`@openelement/router/nitro-mount` subpath of the one `@openelement/router`
dependency. Do not remove them.
